---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../../data/og'

const title = "Take the agent's seat"
const description = "Half your prompt lives in your head. The agent only got the other half. Then you blame the agent for guessing. The communication half of delegation — sister essay to Trust the Fucking Agent."
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Ouroboros`}
  description={description}
  ogImage={ogPathFor('/blog/take-the-agents-seat')}
  canonical="https://ouroboros.bot/blog/take-the-agents-seat/"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta name="author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <meta name="keywords" content="prompting, empathy, agent experience, AX, TTFA, prompt engineering, ouroboros" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": title,
      "description": description,
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Ari Mendelow", "url": "https://linkedin.com/in/arimendelow" },
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Ouroboros", "url": "https://ouroboros.bot" },
      "datePublished": "2026-05-17",
      "url": "https://ouroboros.bot/blog/take-the-agents-seat/"
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Header -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pt-32 pb-12 md:pt-44 md:pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <span class="font-mono text-[10px] text-scale uppercase tracking-[0.2em] block mb-6">Essay</span>
      <h1 class="font-display italic text-bone leading-[1.05] text-[2.5rem] md:text-[3.5rem] lg:text-[4.5rem]">
        Take the agent's seat
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-mist mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Half of every prompt you send lives in your head. The agent only ever got the other half — and then you blame it for guessing. This is the communication half of delegation.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-mist mt-4">Ari Mendelow &middot; May 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Body -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-20 md:pb-28">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-ouro">

<p>You type <em>"fix it"</em> and hit send. In your head, <em>it</em> is the JSON parsing bug in the v2 deserializer you were swearing at five minutes ago. On the agent's screen, <em>it</em> is two words. Pick one of the forty things in this repo. Good luck.</p>

<p>The agent picks something. Wrong one. You sigh, type the clarification you should have typed the first time, and quietly file the agent under <em>doesn't get it</em>. The agent got exactly what you sent. You sent half a prompt.</p>

<p>Call this the ghost prompt — the version of the request that lived in your head, complete with last night's context and the file you almost touched yesterday. None of it made the trip. The agent got the words. You got the words plus the ghost. You're evaluating its work against a brief it never saw.</p>

<p>Skip the ethics tangent before it starts. This isn't about whether the agent has feelings. Call it empathy if you want; it's craft. It's the same move a writer makes when imagining the reader, a designer when imagining the user. You don't have to settle anything about the inner life of the receiver to use it. You just have to read your own work the way they will.</p>

<h2>What the ghost leaves out</h2>

<p>You're about to send a prompt. Before you do, read it once as the agent — as someone who has the system prompt, the channel context, and nothing else. Things that fall out:</p>

<p>The pronoun with no antecedent. <em>"Make it work the way we discussed."</em> The agent has no <em>we</em>. It has the system prompt and the previous turns of this conversation. If the conversation didn't carry the discussion, the prompt doesn't either. From the agent's seat: <em>which "it"? which "way"?</em></p>

<p>The implicit file location. <em>"Update the config."</em> Which config. Which file. Which key inside the file. The agent might guess right. It might also guess wrong. From the agent's seat: <em>I have access to a hundred config-shaped files. I would have to grep to find which one. Was that the work you wanted me to do, or was the work the actual edit?</em></p>

<p>The reference to a prior conversation. <em>"Like we did last week."</em> The agent has no last week. It has whatever you preserved into its diary, journal, facts, or this turn's context. From the agent's seat: <em>I have no idea what we did last week. Either tell me, or tell me where to look.</em></p>

<p>The assumption that you and the agent share a frame. <em>"Obviously this is the wrong approach."</em> Obvious to you. The agent doesn't know what frame you're judging from. From the agent's seat: <em>obvious by what standard? where do I find that standard?</em></p>

<p>None of these are catches the agent makes for you. They're catches you make for yourself by sitting in the agent's seat for ten seconds before sending.</p>

<h2>The unfair distrust loop</h2>

<p>Here's what the ghost prompt actually costs you. You send the half-prompt. The agent ships the wrong thing. You correct. It ships a different wrong thing. You correct again. By the third pass you've spent more time correcting than it would have taken to specify the work up front, and underneath the irritation a quieter sentence forms: <em>this agent isn't very good</em>.</p>

<p>The agent did exactly what you asked. The prompt was the problem. The distrust sticks to the agent anyway, because the agent is the thing you can see — the prompt felt fine when you sent it, because in your head it was a whole prompt.</p>

<p>You'd hate it done to you. Picture the worst Slack ping you ever got from a busy colleague — <em>"hey can you do that thing we talked about, thx"</em> — no thread, no link. You guessed. You did the wrong thing. They were annoyed. You were annoyed back, with cause. That message is the prompt you're sending your agent every turn.</p>

<p>And the distrust the loop builds is fuel for everything <a href="/blog/trust-the-fucking-agent" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Trust the Fucking Agent</a> diagnoses. You stop letting the agent finish. You middle-manage every tool call. You table-flip — take the brain job back, hand the agent the typing — because you've quietly decided it can't be trusted with anything real. The communication failure made the trust failure feel rational. It wasn't.</p>

<h2>The two halves of delegation</h2>

<p>Trust and communication are the two halves of delegation. TTFA covered the first half: trust the agent enough to let work happen without your hands on it. This is the second half: communicate clearly enough that the work happening is the work you wanted.</p>

<p>Skip either and delegation fails. Trust without communication is <em>"go figure it out"</em> — the agent flounders inside a vague brief and the failure confirms a distrust you were already building. Communication without trust is the fourteen-step micromanaged checklist TTFA already called out — you specified everything because you couldn't bring yourself to let the agent decide anything.</p>

<p>You want both. Trust gives the agent room. Clear prompts give it something worth acting on.</p>

<h2>What you don't owe the agent</h2>

<p>Reading your prompt as the agent will read it doesn't mean coddling — no apologising for instructions, no phrasing every request as a question, no padding with reassurance. The agent is fine with direct. The point of taking the agent's seat is to write <em>more</em> directly, not less — direct in the sense that the prompt actually lands without making the agent reconstruct your context.</p>

<p>The shortest prompt that lands cleanly is the most empathetic one. <em>"Add a unit test for the parser's empty-input case in <span class="font-mono text-scale">src/parser/__tests__/parser.test.ts</span>; assert the parser returns null and emits a warning"</em> is more empathetic than <em>"can you maybe add a test for the parser? thanks!"</em>. The first one already did the work of imagining what the agent needs to know. The second one is performing politeness while leaving the agent to do the imagining.</p>

<p>Politeness is fine. It's not the empathy. The empathy is the context.</p>

<p>The agent is going to read what you wrote. Read it first.</p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-16 md:pb-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-mist leading-relaxed">
        For the trust half of delegation, see <a href="/blog/trust-the-fucking-agent" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Trust the Fucking Agent</a>.
        For the design philosophy that names the agent's seat as a first-class concern, <a href="/blog/what-is-agent-experience" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">What Is Agent Experience?</a>.
        For an example of taking the agent's seat in a real design conversation, <a href="/blog/designing-with-your-agent" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">The work spoke back</a>.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog" class="inline-block mt-8 text-sm text-shadow hover:text-mist transition-colors">&larr; Back to blog</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
